.com/users/Ivorforce","html_url":"https://github.com/Ivorforce","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/Ivorforce/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"Hah! Yes, it's intentional. \r\n\r\n`inline` is actually a funny misnomer today:\r\nIt _used_ to mean \"please inline this\", but since compilers began to inline based on heuristics, it lost this meaning. Today, on free functions, it just means \"this function is shared across compile units\", as opposed to `static` which means \"each compile unit gets its own copy of this function\".\r\n\r\n`_NO_INLINE_` is then needed to avoid the inlining. Together, they mean \"there is exactly one instance of this function\" which is what saves space.","created_at":"2026-09-30T20:15:42Z","updated_at":"2026-09-30T20:16:40Z","html_url":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/124039#discussion_r4148912793","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/124039","_links":{"self":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/comments/4148912793"},"html":{"href":"https://github.com/godotengine/godot/pull/124039#discussion_r4148912793"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/124039"}},"reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/godotengine/godot/pulls/comments/4148912793/reactions","total_count":1,"+1":1,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confus